<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Styria</h1><p class="st-meta">970 CE – 1180 CE (approx.) · capital Graz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Carantanian March on the Mur that grew into Styria, elevated to a duchy in 1180 and later inherited by the Babenbergs and Habsburgs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Carantanian March arose around 970 on the south-eastern border of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-carinthia">Duchy of Carinthia</a>, along the Mur, as a frontier against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>. From 1056 it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otakars">Otakar</a> family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a>, and the territory took its name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> (Steiermark), from their castle of Steyr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otakars">Otakar</a> margraves gradually made the march independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, adding lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a> valley. They founded monasteries such as Rein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorau-abbey">Vorau</a> and encouraged colonisation of the hill country. Iron mining at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzberg">Erzberg</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenerz">Eisenerz</a> became a lasting source of wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1180 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> to a duchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-iv-of-styria">Ottokar IV</a>. Having no heir, he concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgenberg-pact">Georgenberg Pact</a> in 1186, leaving Styria to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenberg</a> dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, who inherited in 1192. Styria remained part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> lands until 1918.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-styria.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Styria" width="320" height="277"><figcaption>Image: B. Jezovnik · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_carantania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>970 CE – 1180 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Graz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ottokar II of Styria, Ottokar IV of Styria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German, Slovene</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carantanian March, Steiermark</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1075&amp;focus=margraviate-of-styria" aria-label="Margraviate of Styria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5042 525 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Margraviate of Styria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5362 781L5336 795M5362 781L5362 781"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5336 795h0M5362 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Graz. <a href="/atlas?year=1075&amp;focus=margraviate-of-styria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
